<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dessau</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dessau-Roßlau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dessau is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulde">Mulde</a> joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. It was the residence of the princes of Anhalt-Dessau from 1603 and the capital of the united Duchy of Anhalt from 1863. In the 20th century it was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junkers">Junkers</a> aircraft works and, from 1925 to 1932, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> school of design, whose building by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-gropius">Walter Gropius</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. It was merged with Roßlau in 2007.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dessau.jpg" alt="Dessau" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sven Scharr · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DessauRathaus.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dessau lies on a floodplain where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulde">Mulde</a> flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, and the setting brings yearly floods; the worst, in 2002, nearly covered the Waldersee district. The town is surrounded by numerous parks and palaces, which make it one of the greenest towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> opened on 1 September 1840, and the Dessau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitterfeld">Bitterfeld</a> line, opened on 17 August 1857 and electrified in 1911, was the first fully electrified long-distance railway in Germany.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Dessau was first mentioned in 1213. It became an important centre in 1570, when the Principality of Anhalt was founded with Dessau as the capital of a state within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In 1603 that state was split into four, later five, Anhalts, and Dessau became the capital of the small state of Anhalt-Dessau. Two of the noble lines died out in 1863, and the Duchy of Anhalt was united again. From 1918 to 1945 Dessau was the capital of the Free State of Anhalt.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> school moved to Dessau in 1925 after it was forced to close in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-gropius">Walter Gropius</a>, Paul Klee and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky">Wassily Kandinsky</a> were among its lecturers there. The Nazis' control of the city council forced the closure of the Dessau Bauhaus in 1932. Allied air raids on 7 March 1945 almost completely destroyed the town, six weeks before American troops occupied it. It was rebuilt in concrete-slab architecture and became a major industrial centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>. In 2007 Dessau became part of the municipality of Dessau-Roßlau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dessau is a district of the independent city of Dessau-Roßlau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, and until 1 July 2007 it was an independent city. Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> buildings form part of the World Heritage Site Bauhaus and its Sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, Dessau and Bernau, among them Gropius's school building. Others include the Dessau-Törten Estate of 1926–1928. The Garden Kingdom of Dessau-Wörlitz, an 18th-century landscape garden in the English style, is also a World Heritage Site. The composer Kurt Weill was born in Dessau, as was the philosopher Moses Mendelssohn in 1729.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dessau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It moved to <b>Dessau</b>, where Gropius’s new building opened in late 1926.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1863 they were formally combined as the Duchy of Anhalt, with <b>Dessau</b> as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-anhalt">Duchy of Anhalt</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Joachim Ernst's regent abdicated in November 1918, and the Duchy of Anhalt became a free state, with a democratic constitution adopted in 1919 and <b>Dessau</b> as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-anhalt">Free State of Anhalt</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bauhaus left Weimar for <b>Dessau</b> in 1925 under nationalist pressure.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-thuringia">Free State of Thuringia</a></li><li><blockquote>Anhalt-<b>Dessau</b> was created in the 1603 partition of Anhalt, with Prince John George I receiving Dessau on the Mulde near its junction with the Elbe.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-dessau">Principality of Anhalt-Dessau</a></li><li><blockquote>The principality was divided among the other Anhalt lines, <b>Dessau</b>, Bernburg and Köthen, while Jever passed to Catherine the Great and so to Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-zerbst">Principality of Anhalt-Zerbst</a></li></ul></section>
